### The Safe Logic Checker Sketch 🔍

Can a 5V Arduino Uno read a 3.3V signal coming FROM a 3.3V sensor? **Yes!**
On an Arduino Uno running at 5V, any voltage above **3.0 Volts** is safely recognized as a digital `HIGH`!

```cpp
// Reading a 3.3V sensor on a 5V Arduino Uno pin
const int sensorPin = 2; // Pin 2 configured as input

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(sensorPin, INPUT);
  Serial.println("Logic Compatibility Monitor Active");
}

void loop() {
  int state = digitalRead(sensorPin);

  if (state == HIGH) {
    Serial.println("Input is HIGH (3.3V is safely recognized by the 5V Uno!)");
  } else {
    Serial.println("Input is LOW (0V Ground)");
  }
  delay(1000);
}
```

#### Key Engineering Takeaway:
- **3.3V Out ➔ 5V In**: Safe! (3.3V is high enough for Uno to register as HIGH).
- **5V Out ➔ 3.3V In**: **DANGER!** Must use a voltage divider or level shifter!
